the dream of the overwatch league for me  has always been completely unrelated to  what might happen behind the business of  it if we really want a sports to be  celebrated we have to look at other  forms of entertainment or sports that  are doing things very successfully and  borrow from them and be inspired from  them and go look let's not keep this  thing precious to ourselves it's not the  band that I discovered and I'm the only  one who's allowed to be into let's  really you know put this on the main  stage and have a lot of people start to  enjoy this thing because it's awesome  and everybody deserves to enjoy it  whether it's rallying a cluster of  pixels back and forth in pong chasing  high schools at the arcade in Donkey  Kong or coming together as teams in  first-person shooters for as long as  video games have existed competition has  been a central pillar in recent years  the term eSport has become a popular way  to refer to organized competitions in  games players square off in virtual  arenas for glory while on lookers are  treated to seeing the most popular games  played at the very highest levels but as  an industry eSports is still very much  in its infancy the lion's share of its  history is one of grassroots efforts  small communities brought together by  their love of a game a desire to share  experiences with like-minded fans and  the drive to prove themselves the best  it's only recently that a formalized  industry has formed around these  competitions however although video  games are a hundred billion dollar  business eSports is a very small slice  of that pie the viewership of popular  events such as League of Legends world's  dota twos the International and Evo pale  in comparison to those of traditional  sports for that to change it needs to  break out from the confines of a niche  form of entertainment targeted at the  hardcore enthusiasts and reach a broader  audience for developer blizzard  entertainment the global popularity of  its team-based shooter overwatch has  created the opportunity to do this it  hopes that by infusing competitive  overwatch with the thrill of watching a  live traditional sports event it can  bring eSports to the masses in this  series we're going to be exploring  blizzards vision for overwatch as a  spectator sports looking at how they're  establishing overwatch League as the  premier eSports organization over the  next five episodes will speak to key  figures involved at every level from the  games director and the league's  Commissioner to production staff team  owners players and fans all to get a  better understanding of blizzards  ambitions each episode will focus on one  of the key pillars that Blizzard has put  in place to support its lofty ambitions  and will reflect on what the company has  achieved so far as well as what it hopes  to achieve in the future  [Music]  [Music]  I'm a dancer commissure the overwash  League my first day of loser was on  Halloween in 2014 and I'm a second day  in the office my boss called me in and  said hey check this out and he played  for me the announce trailer for  overwatch and very early on in my  experiences with the game and I think  this was experience that a lot of us had  and certainly when playing the game was  like this game is really really good  this game's gonna do very well people  are gonna want to play this game  competitively we knew when we were  making overwatch as a game that it was  going to be a highly competitive  experience the goal was to make a  competitive 6v6 action shooter that was  out of the gate what we set out to do  now we told ourselves naively we're not  going to worry about this being an  eSport yet just yet we want it to be a  competitive game but it doesn't  necessarily have to be an eSport within  months of having the first playable  versions of the game up and running the  team was already playing it so  competitively that we resigned ourselves  this is going to be an eSport whether we  like it or not there's so much depth to  the game and there's really something  for everybody if you want to be the guy  you can go play Ginji and you can try to  carry if you want to support you can do  that if you want to take you can do that  if you want to do I don't know if you  want to have a turret and be a very  small Swedish man you can do that  anybody who has played of any  first-person shooter can find value in a  game like overwatch right because you  already know there's a familiarity there  you can say oh I know what this game is  you got deep yes you have healers you  have tanks there's a level of comfort  there and familiarity I think at the  core of any  of experience there has to be a sense of  team play and a sense of competing  objectives and that's what you have in  overwatch you have two teams who both  either want the same thing like in the  example of the control map they both  want to take control of the point or in  our other examples where there is an  attack defense scenario going on one  team has an objective to take something  the other team has the objective of  defending it and it creates an immediate  competitive experience add to that high  level of player skill required and a  high level of coordination and it  immediately puts people in sort of a  competitive mindset the similarities  between overwatch and real world sports  such as football soccer and basketball  are immediately apparent a group of  players each fulfilling a specific role  to overcome the differences of an  opposing team and achieve an objective  it makes sense then that when it came to  thinking about how to present overwatch  as an eSport Blizzard looked to the  Premier League NFL NHL and NBA for  inspiration and so as a part of the work  I was doing sort of came up with an idea  that I at the time called the overwatch  Premier League and it was like hey let's  take a page out of the book from  traditional sports and apply that to  overwatch because this game is sort of  perfect for it it's team-based 6p6 its  global it's set on earth like there was  just all these sort of pieces that in my  mind made a lot of sense to build a  sports league for overwatch that looked  like traditional sports when we were  developing overwatch we were really  cognizant of professional sport the  whole time we're making the game and we  were looking at a lot of other shooters  that were out at that time and in most  of those you cared more about what your  personal score was and you didn't care  so much if the team had won or lost and  also taking a real close look at the mo  buffets that we were we were seeing  games like League of Legends dota 2  heroes of the storm we realized that  there was this  high-value in getting the competitive  spirit going in the team aspect of it  all of us across the team have our  different sports that were you know fans  of we have NFL fans NHL fans MLB fans on  the team so as we were creating the game  we talked a lot about the core game  design and how we wanted you to feel at  the end of a match one of the most  emotional moments for me in my life and  this is I'm like a little sad and  embarrassed to say this but I grew up as  a hockey fan and I grew up in Los  Angeles and our hometown team is the Los  Angeles Kings and they had never won a  Stanley Cup until 2012 and my brother  and I who had grown up together being LA  Kings fans and always watching them lose  or not make the playoffs it was so much  personal investment that we had in this  team that the day that they won the  Stanley Cup my brother and I were both  in the stadium we could only afford  seats in the very last row so I was  literally at the the last row of the  Staples Center and then watching the  moment when they hoisted the cup over  their head it stuck with me for months  like it wasn't just like this brief like  happy moment like oh cool my team won  the game it changed me when the LA Kings  won that Stanley Cup and that I was  there with my brother and I'd like to  think that moments like that can and  should be happening to people with  eSports it's no different people just  haven't discovered it yet  in the eyes of Blizzard  emotionally-charged moments like these  are the key to overwatch league success  and its adoption as a spectator sport  that anyone and everyone can enjoy in  traditional sports landmark events such  as Michael Jordan's iconic leap during  the NBA slam dunk contest in 1988 our  lead knocking out Foreman during rumble  in the jungle or the Miracle on Ice had  the power to transcend each sports fan  base the drama involved bridged the gap  between hardcore fans and those that  ordinarily had little to no interest in  the sport making each one a historic  touchstone  [Music]  [Applause]  competitive gaming has its fair share of  similarly iconic moments Daigo Umehara  'he's jaw-dropping counter-attack  against justin wong and evo 2004 the  taipei assassins unexpected reign of  terror at league of legends world's 2012  all the ridiculous series of fountain  hooks during the battle between navi &amp;  tongfu a dota 2 'the 2013 international  are just a few but these have become a  legendary within isolated communities  among people that have intimate  understanding of complex game systems  and have followed disjointed stories  over the course of months and in some  cases years overwatch is played by 40  million people around the world its  design strikes numerous parallels to  traditional team-based sports making the  key moments of drama happening in the  midst of the action easier to connect  with this approachability opens the door  to watching eSports wider than it has  ever been before and the league serves  as a place where stories can be  cultivated and moments of drama can be  showcased on a bigger stage in front of  a broader audience  but while / which breaks down many of  the barriers other games have faced  Blizzard still has multiple hurdles to  overcome relating to public perception  of eSports and eSports athletes before  they can truly realize their vision for  the league eSports is definitely  regarded as lesser than traditional  sports still I think here in the States  and in Western society I think you have  a lot of people who go oh you sit in a  chair and you play video game that's not  a sport that's ridiculous it's kind of a  silly debate to me it's competition it's  entertaining millions of people want to  watch it who cares what you call it  the vast majority of us are physically  incapable of doing what our pro players  do there is actually a physical  component like when you watch carp a  play Widowmaker you physically cannot do  that  that's the counter snipe on the baby  they dock freeze my bow that that goes  down carp a totally going wild on this  Widowmaker right now so you can have the  debate all night long about is it is  there a physical an opponent is it a  sport is not sport I'm past that debate  it's it's like how do we how do we just  continue to do what we're doing and make  it better I really feel like I grew up  in the generation where for a lot of us  it was not looked upon great that you  played games it was actually something  to be slightly embarrassed about you  know gamers were looked at us you know  anti-social nerds and it wasn't heavily  embraced games were looked at as a toy  you know if you were playing games as an  adult that was something to be ashamed  --fill of and I love that we now live in  an era where games are broadly accepted  and looked at as a part of popular  culture they're looked at as art they're  looked at as entertainment they're  looked at as a extremely successful  business and I'd like to think that we  could get there with eSports as well we  all grew up with video games that's all  we know but the generation before us  didn't maybe it's a little bit harder  for them to accept I think for  Millennials and Gen Z it's it's an easy  buy-in you know this is what we know  this is what we do what people often  miss about gaming because people think  gaming is like this like fleeting hobby  it's like this thing I'm gonna do and  I'm a kid and then like I'll grow out of  it but that's not what it is it's  actually like my identity and it's it's  a part of who I am like I spend so much  time doing this playing games I spend so  much time investing in this community  being a part of it like it's only  natural that I want to see who's  despite the intent behind the overwatch  League it's still a gamble for Blizzard  unlike many other games it's skipped the  grassroots phase of cultivating a  dedicated eSports scene opting to invest  heavily in its vision for a new kind of  eSports entertainment that's open to  everyone to that end it is set up an  arena for live matches to take place in  courted celebrity investors to buy into  permanent team franchises and rolled out  ambitious broadcasting plans this has  all been done with thee if you build it  they will come  mindset for Blizzard giving overwatch  fans the means to throw themselves  deeper into their passion is a small  step toward the bigger dream for the  league but if it is to establish the  future of eSports entertainment the real  challenge is reaching beyond just those  people in the next episode of this  series we'll be going behind the scenes  at the blizzard Arena and talking with  the people charged with taking the  fast-paced action of overwatch and  transforming it into a spectator  experience with all the grandeur of  traditional sports  [Music]  you
 

